    "narrative": "Our offensive ceiling today is anchored by **Corbin Carroll** (85.94), who draws an elite matchup against Michael Lorenzen. Given our organizational mandate to prioritize speed, **Corbin Carroll** remains an essential start, bolstered by a 14% park factor and an 8% boost for holding the #2 spot in the order. Similarly, **James Wood** (76.28) provides a high-efficiency floor as our leadoff hitter against Brandon Young, benefiting from a favorable 4.77 xERA and strong xwOBA indicators. While **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (72.72) offer significant upside, the most notable platoon advantage belongs to **Mickey Moniak** (69.42), whose score is heavily inflated by a 14% park benefit and a favorable right-on-right matchup profile that overrides the otherwise stingy 3.66 xERA of Michael Soroka.\n\nThe optimizer\u2019s toughest decision centered on **Kyle Karros** (44.88), who narrowly missed the starting cut. While **Kyle Karros** benefits from the same park environment as **Mickey Moniak**, the heavy negative modifiers for hitting in the #8 hole and the relative strength of the opposing rotation pushed his score into the \"Marginal\" bucket, forcing us to bench him in favor of higher-projected starters. We are carrying some inherent risk with our \"Lineup Pending\" designations for **Dalton Rushing**, **Cole Young**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Corbin Carroll**, and **Josh Naylor**; should these players slot lower than our assumed batting order, their efficiencies will see immediate downward adjustments. Furthermore, while **Brice Turang** (67.62) provides reliable production, his score has been docked 5% due to incoming wind patterns, and **Seiya Suzuki** (63.54) faces a similar headwind, though we are tracking his favorable matchup against Fedde closely as he attempts to exploit the pitcher's slow delivery to boost our stolen base totals.",
